<Note
  track="math"
  slug="vectors-dot-product"
  lede="A vector is a list of numbers that describes one thing, and the dot product is the single operation that asks “how much do two such lists agree?”. A model’s prediction, a search for similar documents and most of what happens inside a language model are dot products. This note makes both ideas concrete with pictures and small numbers."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    The house-price model from the <a href="/ai/notes/ml/what-is-machine-learning/">first ML note</a> had one feature,
    size, and one weight. Real houses have many features: size, number of rooms, age, distance to the station. The
    model’s guess becomes “size times its weight, plus rooms times its weight, plus age times its weight…”. That
    pattern, multiply matching pairs and add them up, is the <strong>dot product</strong>, and it is the single most
    repeated calculation in all of machine learning. A prediction is a dot product. “Find the documents most like this
    question” is a dot product against every document. Inside a language model, the step that decides which earlier
    words matter is a dot product. If you can picture what a dot product measures, half of the diagrams you will meet
    later are already readable.
  </p>
  <p>Questions that are really about this note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Write a model’s prediction from many features as a single expression.”</li>
    <li>“What does the dot product between two <Term id="embedding">embeddings</Term> tell you?”</li>
    <li>“What is the difference between the dot product and cosine similarity?” (Finished in the <a href="/ai/notes/math/norms-distance-cosine/">norms note</a>.)</li>
    <li>“Why is the <Term id="attention">attention</Term> score a dot product, and what does a large value mean?” (Week 8; the dot-product half is here.)</li>
    <li>“What shape is this array, and why did the multiplication fail?”</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      Vectors, their two operations, the dot product and its geometric meaning, and length. No cross products, no
      abstract vector spaces, no proofs. Matrices get the <a href="/ai/notes/math/matrices-and-shapes/">next note</a>;
      norms and cosine similarity the one after.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      <strong>A vector</strong> is a list of numbers that together describe one thing. A recipe card is a vector:
      (2 eggs, 1 cup flour, 1 cup milk). A weather report is a vector: (sunny = 1, cold = 0, tired = 1). A point on a
      map is a vector: (3 blocks east, 2 blocks north). The numbers are the thing’s <em>coordinates</em>, and the list
      can be as long as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The dot product</strong> takes two lists of the same length, multiplies them item by item, and adds the
      results. That one number answers “how much do these two lists agree?”. Where both lists are large, it goes up.
      Where one is large and the other is small or negative, it goes down. The house-price guess from the first ML
      note is exactly this: a list of weights, a list of features, multiplied pairwise and summed.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A list of numbers describing one thing</td><td><span class="term">vector</span></td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{x}" /> or <Math tex="\vec{x}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>One number in the list</td><td><span class="term">component</span> (or element, entry)</td><td><Math tex="x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>How many numbers are in the list</td><td><span class="term">dimension</span></td><td><Math tex="n" />; the vector is “in <Math tex="\mathbb{R}^n" />”</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A plain single number</td><td><span class="term">scalar</span></td><td><Math tex="c" />, <Math tex="\alpha" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multiply pairwise and add</td><td><span class="term">dot product</span> (inner product)</td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}" /> or <Math tex="\mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{b}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>How long the arrow is</td><td><span class="term">norm</span> (length, magnitude)</td><td><Math tex="\lVert \mathbf{a} \rVert" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stretch a vector by a number</td><td><span class="term">scalar multiplication</span></td><td><Math tex="c\,\mathbf{a}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Some of each, added up</td><td><span class="term">linear combination</span></td><td><Math tex="c_1\mathbf{a} + c_2\mathbf{b}" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="vector">What a vector is</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A vector has two faces, and you need both. As a <em>list</em>, it is data: three numbers about today’s weather,
      or 768 numbers describing the word “cat”. As an <em>arrow</em>, it is a picture: start at the origin and move 3
      steps right and 2 steps up. The list is what the computer stores; the arrow is how you think about it. Every
      sentence about “directions”, “angles” and “closeness” in machine learning is the arrow picture applied to the
      list.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={1} caption="One vector, two faces. The list (3, 2) and the arrow that goes 3 right and 2 up are the same object.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 290" role="img" aria-label="A 2-D arrow from the origin to the point (3, 2) with its components marked, next to the column form of the vector">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={grid(one)} stroke-dasharray="2 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(one)}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(one, [0, 0], [3, 2])} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(one, [0, 0], [3, 0])} stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(one, [3, 0], [3, 2])} stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(one, 3)} cy={sy(one, 2)} r="5"></circle>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(one, 1.5)} y={sy(one, 0) + 22} text-anchor="middle">3 across (x₁)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(one, 3) + 10} y={sy(one, 1) + 5}>2 up (x₂)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(one, 3) + 10} y={sy(one, 2) - 10}>(3, 2)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(one, 0) - 8} y={sy(one, 0) + 20} text-anchor="end">0</text>
      <text class="label-red" x={sx(one, 1.3)} y={sy(one, 1.5)}>x</text>
      <text x="560" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26">x = ( 3 )</text>
      <text x="560" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26">( 2 )</text>
      <text class="small" x="560" y="195" text-anchor="middle">the list</text>
      <text class="small" x="560" y="235" text-anchor="middle">np.array([3, 2])</text>
      <text class="small" x="560" y="258" text-anchor="middle">shape (2,)</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    A <span class="term">vector</span> is an ordered list of numbers. We write it in bold, <Math tex="\mathbf{x}" />,
    or with an arrow, <Math tex="\vec{x}" />, and its numbers with subscripts: <Math tex="x_1" /> is the first,
    <Math tex="x_2" /> the second, and so on up to <Math tex="x_n" />. The count <Math tex="n" /> is the vector’s
    <span class="term">dimension</span>. <Math tex="(3, 2)" /> is a 2-dimensional vector; the weather report
    <Math tex="(1, 0, 1)" /> is 3-dimensional; a GPT-2 word embedding is 768-dimensional. “Dimension” here just means
    “how many numbers”, nothing more mysterious. <em>Order matters</em>: <Math tex="(3, 2)" /> and
    <Math tex="(2, 3)" /> are different vectors, just as “2 eggs, 1 cup flour” is a different recipe from “1 egg, 2
    cups flour”.
  </p>
  <p>
    A single plain number, like 5 or −0.3, is called a <span class="term">scalar</span>, to distinguish it from a
    vector. The word comes from “scale”: multiplying a vector by a scalar scales it, as you will see in a moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mathematicians usually write vectors standing up, as <span class="term">columns</span>, and the next note explains
    why that convention matters once matrices arrive. In NumPy a plain vector is a one-dimensional array with shape
    <code>(n,)</code>: one number in the shape means one axis. The 2-D arrow picture only works for 2 or 3 numbers, but
    every rule below works for 768 just the same. You cannot draw a 768-dimensional arrow, and nobody can; you compute
    with the list and borrow the intuition from the picture.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fNk_zzaMoSs"
    title="3Blue1Brown: Vectors | Chapter 1, Essence of linear algebra"
    meta="About 10 minutes. The three views of a vector (physics arrow, computer-science list, mathematician’s object) and why they are one thing. Watch before reading on."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A vector is an ordered list of <Math tex="n" /> real numbers, equivalently a point or arrow in
      <Math tex="n" />-dimensional space. In ML, an example’s features, a model’s weights and the embeddings below
      are all vectors; the dimension is the number of features.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="operations">Adding and scaling vectors</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      There are only two things you can do to vectors directly, and both are what you would guess. To <strong>add</strong>
      two recipe cards, add the eggs to the eggs and the flour to the flour. To <strong>double</strong> a recipe, double
      every number. As arrows: adding means walking the first arrow and then the second; doubling means an arrow twice
      as long in the same direction.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={2} caption="Left: adding (2, 1) and (1, 3) tip to tail lands at (3, 4). Right: scaling (1, 3) by 2 gives (2, 6), same direction, twice the length; scaling by −1 flips it.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 330" role="img" aria-label="Two panels: vector addition tip to tail, and scalar multiplication">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={grid(add)} stroke-dasharray="2 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(add)}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(add, [0, 0], [2, 1])} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(add, [2, 1], [3, 4])} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(add, [0, 0], [3, 4])} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={arrow(add, [0, 0], [1, 3])} stroke-dasharray="6 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={grid(scale)} stroke-dasharray="2 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(scale)}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(scale, [0, 0], [2, 4.9])} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(scale, [0, 0], [1, 3])} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(scale, [0, 0], [-1, -3])} stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(add, 1)} y={sy(add, 0.5) + 20} text-anchor="middle">u = (2, 1)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(add, 2.6) + 8} y={sy(add, 2.5)}>v = (1, 3)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(add, 1.1)} y={sy(add, 2.6)} text-anchor="end">u + v = (3, 4)</text>
      <text x={sx(add, 2)} y={add.y + add.h + 34} text-anchor="middle">addition: tip to tail</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(scale, 1) + 10} y={sy(scale, 3) + 4}>v = (1, 3)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(scale, 2) + 10} y={sy(scale, 4.6)}>2v = (2, 6), off the chart</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(scale, -1) + 6} y={sy(scale, -0.6)} >−v</text>
      <text x={sx(scale, 2)} y={scale.y + scale.h + 34} text-anchor="middle">scaling: stretch or flip</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Addition</strong> is component by component, and only between vectors of the same dimension:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="(2, 1) + (1, 3) = (2 + 1,\ 1 + 3) = (3, 4)" />
  <p>
    <strong>Scalar multiplication</strong> multiplies every component by the same number:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="2 \cdot (1, 3) = (2, 6) \qquad -1 \cdot (1, 3) = (-1, -3) \qquad 0.5 \cdot (1, 3) = (0.5, 1.5)" />
  <p>
    A positive scalar keeps the direction and changes the length; a negative scalar also flips the arrow around.
    Combine the two operations and you get a <span class="term">linear combination</span>: some amount of one vector
    plus some amount of another, <Math tex="c_1\mathbf{u} + c_2\mathbf{v}" />. For example
    <Math tex="3\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{v} = (6, 3) - (1, 3) = (5, 0)" />. This phrase is worth knowing because a model’s
    prediction is a linear combination of its features (the weights are the amounts), and “the span of these
    vectors” means “everything you can reach with linear combinations of them”. That is as far as span needs to go
    for interviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    Subtraction is addition of a flipped vector, and it has a useful picture: <Math tex="\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}" /> is
    the arrow <em>from</em> the tip of <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> <em>to</em> the tip of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" />. So
    the difference between two points is the arrow between them, which is why “distance” in the
    <a href="/ai/notes/math/norms-distance-cosine/">norms note</a> will start from <Math tex="\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}" />.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7RM-ot2NWY"
    title="3Blue1Brown: Linear combinations, span, and basis vectors | Chapter 2"
    meta="About 10 minutes. Watch for the picture of linear combinations; you can let “basis” wash over you for now."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/precalculus/x9e81a4f98389efdf:vectors"
    title="Khan Academy: Vectors (Precalculus)"
    meta="Do the exercise sets on adding vectors, scalar multiplication and vector components. Forty minutes of practice with instant feedback."
  />

  <h2 id="dot">The dot product</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A small decision rule, the kind a model learns. Should I walk to work today? Give each fact about the day a
      weight: sunny counts +3, cold counts −2, tired counts −4. Today is sunny, not cold, and I am tired:
      <Math tex="(1, 0, 1)" />. Multiply each pair and add: <Math tex="3 \cdot 1 + (-2) \cdot 0 + (-4) \cdot 1 = -1" />.
      Negative, so stay home. You just computed a dot product. It is a weighted sum: each input counted as much as
      its weight says, then totalled. That is the whole definition, and everything else in this note is about what
      that number <em>means</em>. (We will call this the <em>walk score</em> below. In week 5 you will meet it again
      under the name <Term id="neuron">neuron</Term>.)
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    The <span class="term">dot product</span> of two vectors of the same dimension multiplies matching components and
    sums the results:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + \dots + a_n b_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i b_i" />
  <p>
    Three things to notice. The result is a <strong>single number</strong> (a scalar), not a vector. Both vectors must
    have the <strong>same dimension</strong>, or there is nothing to pair up. And the <strong>order does not matter</strong>:
    <Math tex="\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a}" />, because each pair is multiplied either
    way round.
  </p>
  <p>Three worked examples, each used again below:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th><Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /></th><th><Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /></th><th>Pairwise products</th><th><Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}" /></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>(3, 4)</td><td>(4, 3)</td><td>12 + 12</td><td>24</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(1, 2)</td><td>(2, −1)</td><td>2 − 2</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(3, −2, −4)</td><td>(1, 0, 1)</td><td>3 + 0 − 4</td><td>−1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    You will also see the dot product written <Math tex="\mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{b}" /> (“a transpose b”) or
    <Math tex="\langle \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b} \rangle" />. All three mean the same sum. The transpose notation comes
    from matrices and is explained in the next note; for now read <Math tex="\mathbf{w}^\top \mathbf{x}" /> as
    <Math tex="\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{x}" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two small but useful special cases. The dot product of a vector with a vector of all ones is its sum:
    <Math tex="(1, 1, 1) \cdot (a, b, c) = a + b + c" />. And the dot product with <Math tex="(1/n, \dots, 1/n)" /> is
    its mean. So averaging is a dot product too, which is one reason the operation shows up in so many places.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The dot product is the sum of element-wise products of two equal-length vectors. It is a scalar, it is
      symmetric, and it is linear in each argument. A linear model’s prediction is a dot product of its weight vector
      with the input features, plus a bias.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="geometry">What the dot product measures</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Picture two arrows from the same point. If they point the same general way, the dot product is positive. If
      they point in opposite directions, it is negative. If they are at right angles, it is exactly zero. And the more
      closely they line up, the bigger it gets. So the dot product is a <em>measure of agreement in direction</em>,
      scaled by how long the arrows are. That is why “similar” in machine learning so often means “large dot product”.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={3} caption="The sign of the dot product tells you the angle. Same side: positive. Right angle: zero. Opposite: negative. Here a = (2, 1) in every panel.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 290" role="img" aria-label="Three panels showing a positive, zero and negative dot product between two arrows">
      {signs.map((s) => (
        <>
          <g class="sketch">
            <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(s.panel)} />
            <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(s.panel, [0, 0], s.a)} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            <path class="stroke" d={arrow(s.panel, [0, 0], s.b)} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
          </g>
          <text class="small label-red" x={sx(s.panel, s.a[0]) + 6} y={sy(s.panel, s.a[1]) + 4}>a</text>
          <text class="small" x={sx(s.panel, s.b[0]) + (s.b[0] < 0 ? -14 : 6)} y={sy(s.panel, s.b[1]) + (s.b[1] < 0 ? 16 : -6)}>b = ({s.b[0]}, {s.b[1]})</text>
          <text x={s.panel.x + s.panel.w / 2} y={s.panel.y + s.panel.h + 30} text-anchor="middle">{s.label}</text>
          <text class="small" x={s.panel.x + s.panel.w / 2} y={s.panel.y + s.panel.h + 52} text-anchor="middle">{s.sub}</text>
        </>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>The exact statement is the single most useful formula in this note:</p>
  <Math block tex="\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = \lVert \mathbf{a} \rVert \, \lVert \mathbf{b} \rVert \, \cos\theta" />
  <p>
    where <Math tex="\lVert \mathbf{a} \rVert" /> is the length of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> (defined properly in the
    next section) and <Math tex="\theta" /> is the angle between the two arrows. Read it as: dot product = (length of
    a) × (length of b) × (how aligned they are). The cosine is the alignment dial. It is 1 when the arrows point the
    same way (<Math tex="\theta = 0°" />), 0 at a right angle (<Math tex="90°" />), and −1 when they point opposite
    ways (<Math tex="180°" />). You do not need any other trigonometry; those three values and “in between means in
    between” are enough.
  </p>
  <p>Check it on the first worked example. <Math tex="\mathbf{a} = (3, 4)" /> and <Math tex="\mathbf{b} = (4, 3)" /> both have length 5 (by Pythagoras: <Math tex="\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5" />). Their dot product was 24, so</p>
  <Math block tex="\cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert} = \frac{24}{5 \cdot 5} = 0.96 \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta \approx 16°" />
  <p>
    Two arrows of the same length, pointing almost the same way: a large dot product, as the picture promised. The
    second example, <Math tex="(1, 2) \cdot (2, -1) = 0" />, says those two arrows are at exactly 90° to each other;
    draw them and you will see it. Vectors with a zero dot product are called <span class="term">orthogonal</span>,
    and the word will come back when the eigenvector note talks about directions that do not interfere with each
    other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The formula also explains a subtlety that trips people up. A dot product can be large because the arrows are
    aligned <em>or</em> because one of them is simply long. Two of the word vectors from <a href="#embeddings">the
    embeddings section</a> can have a big dot product just because one has big numbers in it. When you only care about direction, you divide the lengths out, and what remains is
    <Math tex="\cos\theta" /> itself: <span class="term">cosine similarity</span>. The
    <a href="/ai/notes/math/norms-distance-cosine/">norms note</a> is about exactly when to use which.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LyGKycYT2v0"
    title="3Blue1Brown: Dot products and duality | Chapter 9"
    meta="About 14 minutes. The first half shows why the pairwise-multiply-and-add rule and the projection picture are the same thing. The second half, on duality, is optional."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://betterexplained.com/articles/vector-calculus-understanding-the-dot-product/"
    title="Better Explained: Understanding the dot product"
    meta="The “how much of a goes in the direction of b” explanation with several everyday analogies. Ten minutes."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Geometrically the dot product equals the product of the two lengths times the cosine of the angle between
      them, so it measures alignment: positive when the vectors point the same way, zero when orthogonal, negative
      when opposed. Normalizing out the lengths gives cosine similarity.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="length">Length, unit vectors and projection</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      How long is the arrow (3, 4)? Walk 3 east and 4 north and you are 5 away from where you started, by the
      Pythagoras rule for right triangles. The dot product gives this for free: dot a vector with <em>itself</em> and
      you get the squared length, because <Math tex="3 \cdot 3 + 4 \cdot 4 = 25" />. So length is “square root of
      the self dot product”, and it works in any number of dimensions where you cannot draw the triangle.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>The <span class="term">length</span> (also <span class="term">norm</span> or <span class="term">magnitude</span>) of a vector is</p>
  <Math block tex="\lVert \mathbf{a} \rVert = \sqrt{\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}} = \sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2}" />
  <p>
    For <Math tex="(3, 4)" /> that is <Math tex="\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5" />; for <Math tex="(1, 1, 1, 1)" /> it is
    <Math tex="\sqrt{4} = 2" />. A vector of length exactly 1 is a <span class="term">unit vector</span>; it carries a
    direction and nothing else. You make one by dividing a vector by its own length, which is called
    <span class="term">normalizing</span>: <Math tex="(3, 4) / 5 = (0.6, 0.8)" />, and indeed
    <Math tex="0.36 + 0.64 = 1" />. Models that produce the word vectors of <a href="#embeddings">the embeddings
    section</a> often normalize their outputs to unit length so that dot products between them are pure cosines.
  </p>
  <Figure n={4} caption="Projection. The shadow that a = (3, 4) casts onto the direction of b = (4, 3) has length (a·b)/‖b‖ = 24/5 = 4.8. The dot product is “how much of a points along b”, times the length of b.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 320" role="img" aria-label="Vector a projected onto vector b with the perpendicular dropped from the tip of a">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={grid(proj)} stroke-dasharray="2 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(proj)}></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={arrow(proj, [0, 0], [projB[0] * 1.2, projB[1] * 1.2])} stroke-dasharray="4 6"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(proj, [0, 0], projB)} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(proj, [0, 0], projA)} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(proj, projA, projPoint)} stroke-dasharray="6 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(proj, [0, 0], projPoint)} stroke-width="6" opacity="0.35"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(proj, projPoint[0])} cy={sy(proj, projPoint[1])} r="5"></circle>
      </g>
      <text class="label-red" x={sx(proj, 3) - 26} y={sy(proj, 4) - 4}>a = (3, 4)</text>
      <text x={sx(proj, 4) + 10} y={sy(proj, 3) + 6}>b = (4, 3)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(proj, 2.1)} y={sy(proj, 1.2)}>shadow, length 4.8</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(proj, 3.55)} y={sy(proj, 3.6)}>right angle</text>
      <text class="small" x="470" y="120">a·b = ‖a‖ ‖b‖ cos θ</text>
      <text class="small" x="470" y="150">= (shadow of a on b) × ‖b‖</text>
      <text class="small" x="470" y="180">= 4.8 × 5 = 24</text>
      <text class="small" x="470" y="226">shadow = a·b / ‖b‖</text>
      <text class="small" x="470" y="256">= 24 / 5 = 4.8</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    Fig. 4 is the second way to read a dot product, and it is the one that makes trained models make sense. Drop a
    perpendicular from the tip of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> onto the line through <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" />. The piece
    of that line from the origin to the foot of the perpendicular is the <span class="term">projection</span> of
    <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> onto <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" />, the shadow <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> would cast if light
    shone straight down onto <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" />. Its length is
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\text{shadow} = \lVert \mathbf{a} \rVert \cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert \mathbf{b} \rVert}" />
  <p>
    If <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> is a unit vector, the shadow is simply <Math tex="\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}" />: the
    dot product with a unit vector measures <em>how much of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> lies along that direction</em>.
    This is what a linear model does. Its weight vector <Math tex="\mathbf{w}" /> is a direction in the space of
    features, and <Math tex="\mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{x}" /> asks how far the input <Math tex="\mathbf{x}" /> extends
    along that direction. Training moves <Math tex="\mathbf{w}" /> until the direction it points in is the one that
    matters for the task.
  </p>
  <p>
    One fact to file away: a dot product can never exceed the product of the two lengths,
    <Math tex="|\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}| \le \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />, because
    <Math tex="|\cos\theta| \le 1" />. It is called the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, and its only job in these notes is
    to guarantee that cosine similarity always lies between −1 and 1.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The norm is the square root of a vector’s dot product with itself. Dividing by the norm gives a unit vector,
      and the dot product with a unit vector is the length of the projection onto that direction. A linear model’s
      weight vector defines a direction; its score measures how far the input projects along it.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="everywhere">Why the dot product is everywhere in ML</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Almost every model answers the same question over and over: “how well does this input match this pattern?”.
      Store the pattern as a vector, store the input as a vector, and the dot product is the match score. Train the
      pattern vectors, and you have a model. Compare two inputs instead, and you have a similarity search. Compare
      every word in a sentence with every other word, and you have the heart of a language model (week 8 calls it
      <Term id="attention">attention</Term>).
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Here is the same operation wearing five different names. In each row the thing being computed is one dot product.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Where</th><th>The dot product</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Linear regression (week 1)</td><td><Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" /></td><td>The prediction is a weighted sum of the features.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Yes/no prediction (week 3)</td><td><Math tex="\sigma(\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b)" /></td><td>Same score, squashed into a probability by the S-curve (<Term id="sigmoid">sigmoid</Term>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A <Term id="neuron">neuron</Term> (week 5)</td><td><Math tex="f(\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b)" /></td><td>Same score, passed through a simple bend.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedding similarity (below; week 11)</td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{e}_{\text{cat}} \cdot \mathbf{e}_{\text{dog}}" /></td><td>How alike two things are, as learned by a model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Term id="attention">Attention</Term> (week 8)</td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{k}" /></td><td>How much one word in a sentence should look at another.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Choosing the next <Term id="token">token</Term> (week 8)</td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{h}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\text{token}}" /></td><td>How well the model’s current state matches each possible next word.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Why is the dot product the match score, rather than something else? Because it has the geometric meaning you just
    saw (big when aligned), because it is cheap (one multiply and one add per component, which GPUs do by the
    trillion), and because it is <em>linear</em>: doubling a component of <Math tex="\mathbf{w}" /> exactly doubles its
    contribution. Linearity is what makes the calculus in week 2 easy: nudge <Math tex="w_i" /> by a tiny amount and
    <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}" /> moves by exactly <Math tex="x_i" /> times that amount, nothing more
    complicated. Hold onto that sentence; it is the reason training a linear model is simple.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Linear models, similarity search and, later, neurons and attention scores all compute a dot product between a
      learned vector and an input vector. It is the natural match score: it measures alignment, it is linear so its
      slopes are trivial, and it maps directly onto matrix-multiply hardware.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="embeddings">Vectors that mean something: embeddings</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Suppose you had to describe words with numbers. You might give each word a score for “is an animal”, a score for
      “is a vehicle”, a score for “is small”, and so on. “Cat” would score high on animal and small; “truck” high on
      vehicle and low on small. Each word becomes a list of scores, a vector, and words with similar meanings end up
      with similar lists. A model does this automatically, with hundreds of scores whose meanings nobody chose, and
      the lists are called embeddings. The dot product between two of them is a similarity score.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={5} caption="Toy word vectors in two dimensions. Animal words cluster, vehicle words cluster, and the angle between cat and car is large, so their dot product is small.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Five word vectors drawn as arrows: cat, dog and kitten point one way, car and truck another">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(words)}></path>
        {wordVecs.map((w) => (
          <path class={`stroke ${w.w === 'cat' || w.w === 'car' ? 'ink-red' : ''}`} d={arrow(words, [0, 0], w.v)} stroke-width={w.w === 'cat' || w.w === 'car' ? 3 : 2} marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
        ))}
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${sx(words, 0.28)} ${sy(words, 0.093)} A ${0.3 * words.w / 1.1} ${0.3 * words.h / 1.1} 0 0 0 ${sx(words, 0.065)} ${sy(words, 0.29)}`} stroke-dasharray="4 4"></path>
      </g>
      {wordVecs.map((w) => (
        <text class={`small ${w.w === 'cat' || w.w === 'car' ? 'label-red' : ''}`} x={sx(words, w.v[0]) + 8} y={sy(words, w.v[1]) + 4}>
          {w.w}
        </text>
      ))}
      <text class="small" x={sx(words, 0.22)} y={sy(words, 0.2)}>θ ≈ 59°</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(words, 1.1) + 6} y={sy(words, 0) + 5}>“vehicle-ness”</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(words, 0)} y={words.y - 10}>“animal-ness”</text>
      <text class="small" x="540" y="200">cat·dog = 0.855</text>
      <text class="small" x="540" y="226">cat·car = 0.45</text>
      <text class="small" x="540" y="252">cat·kitten = 0.915</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    An <span class="term">embedding</span> is a vector that a model has learned to represent something: a word, a
    sentence, an image, a user. The defining property is that <em>similar things get vectors that point in similar
    directions</em>, so that the dot product (or cosine) between embeddings behaves like a similarity score. In Fig. 5,
    cat and kitten are nearly parallel; cat and car are far apart. With real embeddings the dimension is 384, 768 or
    more, and the axes have no human names, but the arithmetic is identical.
  </p>
  <p>
    The famous demonstration is that vector arithmetic on word embeddings captures relationships: with the right
    embeddings, <Math tex="\mathbf{e}_{\text{king}} - \mathbf{e}_{\text{man}} + \mathbf{e}_{\text{woman}}" /> lands
    close to <Math tex="\mathbf{e}_{\text{queen}}" />. That is vector subtraction and addition from Fig. 2 doing
    something that looks like reasoning. It works approximately, not exactly, and only for some relationships, but it
    is the standard illustration that directions in embedding space can carry meaning.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also the entire basis of the retrieval systems in weeks 11 and 12: embed every document, embed the
    question, and return the documents whose vectors have the largest dot product with the question’s vector. A
    vector database is a system for doing that dot product against millions of vectors quickly.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-word2vec/"
    title="Jay Alammar: The Illustrated Word2vec"
    meta="Read the first half, up to and including the personality-score analogy and the king − man + woman example. It is this section with better pictures. The training details can wait until week 7."
  />
  <Source
    kind="try"
    href="https://projector.tensorflow.org/"
    title="TensorFlow Embedding Projector"
    meta="Real word embeddings, squashed to 3-D so you can look at them. Search for a word and see its nearest neighbours by cosine. Five minutes makes the idea permanent."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “An embedding is a learned dense vector where geometric closeness reflects semantic similarity. Similarity is
      measured with the dot product or cosine; retrieval systems embed the query and return the stored vectors with
      the highest scores.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="many">Many dot products at once</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Often you want several scores from the same input: “should I walk?” and “is the weather good?”, each with its
      own weights. That is many dot products with the same <Math tex="\mathbf{x}" />. Stack the weight vectors as rows
      of a table and all the scores become one operation: table times vector. The next note is about those tables,
      but the idea is already here, so see it once now.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Two scores on the walk inputs, written as two dot products and then as one matrix-vector product:</p>
  <Math block tex="\mathbf{w}_1 \cdot \mathbf{x} = (3, -2, -4)\cdot(1, 0, 1) = -1 \qquad \mathbf{w}_2 \cdot \mathbf{x} = (1, -1, 0)\cdot(1, 0, 1) = 1" />
  <Math block tex={String.raw`\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -2 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}`} />
  <p>
    Each row of the matrix dotted with the column gives one entry of the result. That is <em>all</em> a matrix-vector
    product is: a stack of dot products. If you understand this note, the next one is bookkeeping about which rows
    pair with which columns and what shape comes out.
  </p>
  <NewInCode items={[
      ["np.array([[...], [...]])", "A list of lists makes a 2-D array: a table with one inner list per row.", "numpy/array"],
      ["W @ x", "With a table on the left, @ takes the dot product of every row with x and gives one number per row.", "numpy/matmul"],
      [".shape", "The size along each axis: (2, 3) means 2 rows and 3 columns; (3,) means a plain list of 3 numbers.", "numpy/shape"],
    ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={matrixPreviewCode} />

  <h2 id="code">Code</h2>
  <p>Type these out. Each one is a sentence from above turned into NumPy.</p>
  <h3>Vectors, shapes, addition, scaling, and the walk score</h3>
  <NewInCode items={[
      [".ndim", "How many axes the array has: 1 for a vector, 2 for a table.", "numpy/shape"],
      ["w * inputs", "* on two arrays of the same length multiplies matching components and gives a new array. It does not add them up.", "numpy/arith"],
      ["np.sum(a)", "Adds up all the numbers in the array.", "numpy/sum"],
      ["np.dot(a, b)", "The dot product of two vectors. a @ b is the same thing.", "numpy/dot"],
    ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={basicsCode} />
  <h3>The dot product three ways, the angle, and the projection</h3>
  <NewInCode items={[
      ["np.sqrt(x)", "The square root.", "numpy/sqrt"],
      ["np.linalg.norm(v)", "The length of a vector: the square root of v · v.", "numpy/linalg-norm"],
      ["np.arccos(c) and np.degrees(r)", "arccos turns a cosine back into an angle, measured in radians; degrees converts radians to degrees.", "numpy/trig"],
    ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={dotCode} />
  <h3>Toy embeddings and nearest-neighbour search</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={embedCode} />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.dot.html"
    title="NumPy documentation: numpy.dot"
    meta="Read the first three bullet points: what dot does for two 1-D arrays, for 2-D arrays, and when it broadcasts. The @ operator is the modern spelling of the same thing for 1-D and 2-D inputs."
  />

  <h2 id="later">Where you will meet this again</h2>
  <Callout kind="note" label="Optional preview">
    <p>
      A look ahead, so the dot product is an old friend when it returns. Unfamiliar words link to the glossary. Skip
      on a first read if you like.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tomorrow, linear regression.</strong> The house model with many features is
      <Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />, and the whole dataset’s predictions are one
      matrix-vector product, exactly as in <a href="#many">the section above</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 5, neural networks.</strong> A <Term id="neuron">neuron</Term> is the walk score with a bend
      applied to the result; a <Term id="layer">layer</Term> is many of them, which is the matrix-vector product
      again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 7, embeddings inside a language model.</strong> Every <Term id="token">token</Term> the model
      knows has a vector of 768 numbers in a lookup table. Feeding text to the model starts by replacing each token
      with its vector, so a sentence becomes a stack of vectors.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 8, attention.</strong> For each pair of tokens the model computes the dot product of one token’s
      <em>query</em> vector with the other’s <em>key</em> vector. A large value means “pay attention to this one”.
      That is Fig. 3 applied to every pair of positions in the sentence. At the very end, the model’s state vector is
      dotted with every token’s embedding; the biggest dot product is its best guess for the next word.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 11 and 12, retrieval.</strong> A <Term id="rag">RAG</Term> system turns documents and questions
      into vectors with an embedding model and ranks documents by dot product or cosine similarity with the question.
      That ranking is the “R” in RAG.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The question usually arrives as “what does the dot product tell you?” or “why is similarity a dot product?”. Here
    is a complete answer in about seventy seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “The dot product of two equal-length vectors is the sum of their element-wise products. Algebraically it is a
      weighted sum, which is why a linear model’s prediction is <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />, and
      so is the weighted sum inside a neuron. Geometrically it equals the product of the two norms times the
      cosine of the angle between the vectors, so it measures alignment: positive for vectors pointing the same way,
      zero for orthogonal ones, negative for opposed ones. Equivalently it is the length of one vector’s projection
      onto the other, scaled by the other’s length.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why it is used as a similarity score between embeddings, and later as the attention score between a
      query and a key: a large dot product means the two vectors agree in direction. When vector lengths vary and only
      direction matters, we divide the lengths out and use cosine similarity, which lies between −1 and 1. And because
      the dot product is linear in each argument, its slopes are simple, and a whole stack of dot products is one
      matrix-vector multiply, which is what GPUs are built for.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Dot product or cosine for embeddings?” Cosine if lengths are not meaningful or not normalized; dot product if the embedding model was trained for it or vectors are already unit length (then they are the same).</li>
    <li>“If <Math tex="w_i" /> changes by a small amount, how much does <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}" /> change?” By <Math tex="x_i" /> times that amount. (Week 2 calls this the <Term id="derivative">derivative</Term>, and it is why each weight’s update is proportional to the input it multiplies.)</li>
    <li>“Why do we scale <Term id="attention">attention</Term> scores by <Math tex="\sqrt{d}" />?” (Week 8.) Dot products of long random vectors grow with the dimension; dividing keeps them on a fixed scale. Problem 14 below shows the numbers.</li>
    <li>“Can two different vectors have the same dot product with a third?” Yes, infinitely many: all vectors with the same projection onto it. The dot product throws away everything perpendicular to the other vector.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="What is a vector, in one sentence each for a mathematician and for a programmer?">
    <p>
      Mathematician: an element of <Math tex="\mathbb{R}^n" />, a point or arrow in <Math tex="n" />-dimensional
      space. Programmer: a one-dimensional array of <Math tex="n" /> numbers with shape <code>(n,)</code>. Same object.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is the dot product of two vectors a vector or a number?">
    <p>A number (scalar). The element-wise product <Math tex="a_i b_i" /> is a vector; summing it gives the dot product.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does a dot product of zero mean?">
    <p>
      The vectors are orthogonal: at 90° to each other, with no component in common. Neither tells you anything about
      the other’s direction.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why is a linear model’s score called a weighted sum, and why is that a dot product?">
    <p>
      Each feature is multiplied by its own weight and the products are summed:
      <Math tex="\sum_i w_i x_i" />. That is the definition of <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}" />. The bias
      <Math tex="b" /> is added afterwards.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Two embeddings have a large dot product. Name two different reasons that could be true.">
    <p>
      They point in nearly the same direction (high cosine), or one of them is simply long (large norm). Cosine
      similarity removes the second cause.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How do you get a vector’s length from the dot product?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert = \sqrt{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}}" />. For <Math tex="(3, 4)" />:
      <Math tex="\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is a unit vector and how do you make one?">
    <p>
      A vector of length 1. Divide any non-zero vector by its norm. <Math tex="(3, 4)/5 = (0.6, 0.8)" />. It keeps
      the direction and discards the length.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does the dot product with a unit vector measure?">
    <p>
      The length of the projection onto that direction: how much of the vector lies along it. For a general vector
      <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> the projection length is <Math tex="(\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b})/\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why can cosine similarity never be bigger than 1?">
    <p>
      Cosine similarity is <Math tex="\cos\theta" />, and the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality says
      <Math tex="|\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}| \le \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />, so the ratio is
      between −1 and 1.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is a linear combination, and where does one appear in machine learning?">
    <p>
      A sum of vectors each scaled by a number: <Math tex="c_1\mathbf{u} + c_2\mathbf{v}" />. A linear model’s
      prediction is a linear combination of the features (the weights are the amounts). Later: a layer’s output is a
      linear combination of its inputs, and attention’s output is a linear combination of “value” vectors.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does a matrix-vector product have to do with dot products?">
    <p>
      Each entry of the result is the dot product of one row of the matrix with the vector. A matrix-vector product
      is a stack of dot products done at once, one per row.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="In NumPy, what is the difference between w * x and w @ x?">
    <p>
      <code>w * x</code> is element-wise: a vector of products. <code>w @ x</code> (or <code>np.dot</code>) sums
      those products: the dot product, a scalar. <code>np.sum(w * x)</code> equals <code>w @ x</code>.
    </p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first, then check. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. Let u = (2, −1, 3) and v = (0, 4, −2). Compute u + v, 3u, u − v and 2u + 0.5v.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(2, 3, 1)" />; <Math tex="(6, -3, 9)" />; <Math tex="(2, -5, 5)" />; <Math tex="(4, 0, 5)" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Compute the dot products: (1, 2)·(3, 4); (1, 2, 3)·(4, 5, 6); (2, −1, 3)·(0, 4, −2); (1, 1, 1, 1)·(5, −2, 7, 0).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="3 + 8 = 11" />; <Math tex="4 + 10 + 18 = 32" />; <Math tex="0 - 4 - 6 = -10" />;
      <Math tex="5 - 2 + 7 + 0 = 10" /> (the all-ones vector sums the other).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. For each pair, say without computing the angle whether it is acute, right or obtuse: (1, 2) and (2, 1); (1, 2) and (−2, 1); (1, 2) and (−1, −3).">
    <p>
      Dot products 4, 0, −7. Acute (positive), right (zero), obtuse (negative).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Find x so that (1, 2, x) is orthogonal to (3, −1, 2).">
    <p>
      Set the dot product to zero: <Math tex="3 - 2 + 2x = 0" />, so <Math tex="x = -0.5" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Compute the lengths of (3, 4), (1, 1, 1, 1), (5, 12) and (0.6, 0.8). Which is a unit vector?">
    <p>
      5; 2; 13; 1. The last one is a unit vector (it is <Math tex="(3, 4)/5" />).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. Normalize (2, −2, 1). Check the result has length 1.">
    <p>
      Length <Math tex="\sqrt{4 + 4 + 1} = 3" />, so the unit vector is <Math tex="(2/3, -2/3, 1/3)" />. Check:
      <Math tex="4/9 + 4/9 + 1/9 = 1" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. Find the cosine of the angle between (1, 0) and (1, 1), and between (1, 2, 2) and (2, 1, −2).">
    <p>
      First: dot 1, lengths 1 and <Math tex="\sqrt{2}" />, so <Math tex="\cos\theta = 1/\sqrt{2} = 0.707" /> (45°).
      Second: dot <Math tex="2 + 2 - 4 = 0" />, so <Math tex="\cos\theta = 0" /> (90°), orthogonal.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. What is the projection length of a = (6, 2) onto b = (3, 4)? And the projection of b onto a?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 18 + 8 = 26" />. Onto <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" />:
      <Math tex="26 / \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert = 26/5 = 5.2" />. Onto <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" />:
      <Math tex="26 / \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert = 26/\sqrt{40} = 4.11" />. Projection is not symmetric; the dot product is.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. The walk score has weights (3, −2, −4) and an added constant (bias) of −1. Write it as a dot product plus the bias and evaluate it for the days (1, 1, 0), (0, 0, 1) and (1, 0, 0).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="z = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />. Days: <Math tex="3 - 2 + 0 - 1 = 0" />;
      <Math tex="-4 - 1 = -5" />; <Math tex="3 - 1 = 2" />. The first day sits exactly on the cutoff between “walk”
      and “stay home”.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. Toy embeddings: cat = (0.9, 0.8, 0.1), dog = (0.8, 0.9, 0.2), car = (0.1, 0.2, 0.9), bus = (0.2, 0.1, 0.8). Which pair is most similar by dot product? Compute all six.">
    <p>
      cat·dog 1.46, cat·car 0.34, cat·bus 0.34, dog·car 0.44, dog·bus 0.41, car·bus 0.76. Most similar: cat and
      dog. Next: car and bus. The animals and the vehicles form two clusters.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. Show algebraically that (a + b)·(a + b) = ‖a‖² + 2 a·b + ‖b‖². What does this say about the length of a sum when a and b are orthogonal?">
    <p>
      Expand using linearity: <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{b}" />,
      and <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = \mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{a}" />. If they are orthogonal the middle term
      vanishes and <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert^2" />:
      Pythagoras, in any dimension.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. Write the mean of (4, 8, 6) as a dot product.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)\cdot(4, 8, 6) = 18/3 = 6" />. Any weighted average is a dot product with a weight
      vector whose entries sum to 1.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Implement dot, norm, normalize and cosine_similarity from scratch with plain loops. Test them against NumPy on 1000 random pairs of 50-dimensional vectors.">
    <NewInCode items={[
        ["np.random.default_rng(0)", "Makes a random-number generator. The 0 is the seed: it makes the generator give the same numbers on every run.", "numpy/default-rng"],
        ["rng.normal(size=50)", "50 random numbers, clustered around 0 and mostly between −2 and 2.", "numpy/rng-normal"],
        ["assert condition", "Stops the program with an error if the condition is false: a built-in self-check.", "python/assert"],
        ["np.isclose(x, y)", "True when two numbers are equal up to tiny rounding errors. Use it instead of == for decimals.", "numpy/isclose"],
      ]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

# Plain loops only: no NumPy inside these four functions.
def dot(a, b):
    total = 0.0
    for i in range(len(a)):
        total += a[i] * b[i]
    return total

def norm(a):
    return dot(a, a) ** 0.5            # ** 0.5 is the square root

def normalize(a):
    n = norm(a)
    result = []
    for value in a:
        result.append(value / n)
    return result

def cosine_similarity(a, b):
    return dot(a, b) / (norm(a) * norm(b))

# Test against NumPy on 1000 random pairs of 50-number vectors.
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
for trial in range(1000):
    a = rng.normal(size=50)
    b = rng.normal(size=50)
    assert np.isclose(dot(a, b), a @ b)
    assert np.isclose(norm(a), np.linalg.norm(a))
    assert np.isclose(np.linalg.norm(normalize(a)), 1.0)
    numpy_cosine = (a @ b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))
    assert np.isclose(cosine_similarity(a, b), numpy_cosine)
    # cosine always lies between -1 and 1
    assert -1.0 <= cosine_similarity(a, b) <= 1.0
print("all good")     # all good`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Generate 10,000 pairs of random vectors in 2, 10, 100 and 1000 dimensions (standard normal entries). For each dimension, print the mean and spread (standard deviation) of the dot product. What happens as the dimension grows? (A later week relies on this fact; you do not need that part yet.)">
    <NewInCode items={[
        ["rng.normal(size=(10000, d))", "A table of random numbers with 10,000 rows and d columns: one random vector per row.", "numpy/rng-normal"],
        ["np.sum(a * b, axis=1)", "axis=1 adds across each row instead of adding everything, so you get one total per row.", "numpy/sum"],
        [".mean() and .std()", "The average of an array, and its standard deviation: how far a typical value sits from that average. Taught properly in week 3.", "numpy/std"],
      ]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
for d in [2, 10, 100, 1000]:
    # 10,000 random vectors of length d, one per row
    a = rng.normal(size=(10000, d))
    b = rng.normal(size=(10000, d))
    # add across each row: one dot product per pair
    dots = np.sum(a * b, axis=1)
    mean = dots.mean()                  # the average dot product
    # how far a typical dot product is from that average
    spread = dots.std()
    ratio = spread / np.sqrt(d)
    print("d =", d, " mean =", round(mean, 2), " spread =", round(spread, 2),
          " spread / sqrt(d) =", round(ratio, 2))
# d = 2      mean = 0.01    spread = 1.42    spread / sqrt(d) = 1.01
# d = 10     mean = 0.03    spread = 3.16    spread / sqrt(d) = 1.0
# d = 100    mean = -0.03   spread = 9.93    spread / sqrt(d) = 0.99
# d = 1000   mean = 0.35    spread = 31.86   spread / sqrt(d) = 1.01
# The average stays near 0, but the spread grows like the square root of the
# dimension:
# 100 times more numbers gives about 10 times bigger dot products.`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="15. Build a tiny nearest-neighbour search: 1,000 random 'document' vectors of dimension 64 and one query. Return the top 5 by dot product and by cosine similarity. Make one document vector 10× longer and see which ranking changes.">
    <NewInCode items={[
        ["rng.normal(size=(1000, 64))", "A table of random numbers: 1000 rows, 64 columns. Each row is one document's vector.", "numpy/rng-normal"],
        ["docs @ query", "With a table on the left, @ gives one dot product per row, as in “Many dot products at once” above.", "numpy/matmul"],
        ["np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)", "The length of every row, rather than one length for the whole table.", "numpy/linalg-norm"],
        ["np.argsort(scores)", "The positions of the scores in increasing order. Sorting -scores instead gives the largest first.", "numpy/sort"],
        ["order[:5]", "The first five elements. A slice stops just before position 5.", "python/slicing-steps"],
        ["docs[7]", "Row 7 of the table, which is the eighth row because counting starts at 0.", "numpy/slicing"],
      ]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
# 1000 "documents", one 64-number vector per row
docs = rng.normal(size=(1000, 64))
query = rng.normal(size=64)            # one query vector

def top5(scores):
    # positions, largest score first (the minus flips the order)
    order = np.argsort(-scores)
    return order[:5]                   # the first five positions

# one dot product per row: 1000 scores
dot_scores = docs @ query
# the length of every row
lengths = np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)
cos_scores = dot_scores / (lengths * np.linalg.norm(query))
print("dot   ", top5(dot_scores))      # [834 471 525 942 916]
print("cosine", top5(cos_scores))      # [834 471 126 525 236]

# make document 7's vector ten times longer
docs[7] = docs[7] * 10
dot_scores = docs @ query
lengths = np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)
cos_scores = dot_scores / (lengths * np.linalg.norm(query))
# document 7 jumps to the top
print("dot after   ", top5(dot_scores))
# prints [  7 834 471 525 942]
# unchanged: ignores length
print("cosine after", top5(cos_scores))
# prints [834 471 126 525 236]`}
    />
  </QA>
