  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Expecting the dot product to be a vector.</strong> It is one number. The vector of pairwise products is
      the element-wise (Hadamard) product, written <Math tex="\mathbf{a} \odot \mathbf{b}" /> or <code>a * b</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reading a negative dot product as “small similarity”.</strong> It means <em>opposed</em>: the vectors
      point away from each other. Zero is “unrelated”; negative is “anti-related”.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Treating a big dot product as high similarity without checking lengths.</strong> A long vector has big
      dot products with everything. Normalize or use cosine when lengths are not meaningful.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Confusing shape <code>(3,)</code> with <code>(3, 1)</code> or <code>(1, 3)</code>.</strong> The first is
      a vector; the others are matrices with one column or one row. <code>a @ b</code> works for two <code>(3,)</code>
      arrays and gives a scalar; mix in a <code>(3, 1)</code> and you get a <code>(1, 1)</code> matrix or an error.
      The next note is largely about this.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Thinking vectors live only in 2-D or 3-D.</strong> Those are for drawing. Every formula here holds in
      768 dimensions; what changes is only that you can no longer picture the arrow, so you lean on the algebra.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Projection is not symmetric.</strong> The shadow of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> on <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" />
      is <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}/\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />; the shadow of <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> on
      <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> divides by <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert" /> instead. The dot product itself is
      symmetric.
    </p>
  </Callout>

