  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>Vector: ordered list of <Math tex="n" /> numbers = arrow in <Math tex="n" />-D. Shape <code>(n,)</code>. Order matters.</li>
      <li>Add component-wise (tip to tail); scale by a number (stretch, flip if negative). Linear combination: <Math tex="c_1\mathbf{u} + c_2\mathbf{v}" />.</li>
      <li>Dot product: <Math tex="\sum_i a_i b_i" />. A scalar. Symmetric. Needs equal dimensions.</li>
      <li>Meaning: <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert\cos\theta" />. Positive = aligned, 0 = orthogonal, negative = opposed.</li>
      <li>Length: <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert = \sqrt{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}}" />. Unit vector = divide by length. Projection of <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> on <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> = <Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}/\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />.</li>
      <li>Big dot product: aligned, <em>or</em> one vector is long. Cosine similarity removes length.</li>
      <li>Linear regression, embedding similarity, and later neurons and attention scores: all <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}" />.</li>
      <li>Nudge <Math tex="w_i" /> by a little and <Math tex="\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}" /> moves by <Math tex="x_i" /> times that little (week 2 names this the derivative).</li>
      <li>Matrix-vector product = one dot product per row.</li>
      <li>NumPy: <code>w * x</code> is element-wise, <code>w @ x</code> is the dot product.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
